excerpt: 'This paper proposes a measure for industry-level technological complexity using a novel Network Diversity Score, which captures the connections between fundamental knowledge components. This approach outperforms other complexity metrics in key tests. The findings show that technological complexity rose steadily at both technology sector and NAICS industry levels until the Covid-19 pandemic, after which labor-intensive industries experienced a sharp decline. It is also found that the drives of technological complexity differ between less and more competitive markets. Technological complexity is driven more by innovation incentives and financial flexibility in competitive industries, while weaker competitive pressure in concentrated industries reduces firms’ incentives to invest in technological development. The results also show that patent-value measures appear to reward higher complexity more strongly, whereas stock-return outcomes respond positively to complexity only up to a certain point.'
